      "passage": "<p>(1) In recent years, electric cars have moved from science fiction to everyday streets. Supporters describe them as a clean solution to air pollution and climate change. However, the picture is more complex than advertisements suggest.</p><p>(2) The main advantage is that electric cars produce no exhaust fumes. In crowded cities, this could greatly improve air quality and reduce noise. Electric motors are also more efficient than petrol engines, and they require less maintenance because they have fewer moving parts.</p><p>(3) Yet the environmental benefits depend on how the electricity is produced. In countries that rely on coal, charging an electric car may create almost as much pollution as driving a petrol car, simply because the power comes from a dirty source. The batteries also require minerals such as lithium and cobalt, and mining them can damage landscapes and use large amounts of water.</p><p>(4) Another challenge is charging. A driver who lives in an apartment without a private parking space may find it difficult to charge a car overnight. Fast charging stations are still limited in many areas, and charging a car can take much longer than filling a tank with petrol. This is why some families choose hybrid cars, which use both electricity and petrol.</p><p>(5) Electric cars are therefore an important tool, not a magic solution. Their real value will depend on cleaner electricity, better public transport and changes in how people travel. A city with fewer cars, well designed for walking and cycling, may do more for the environment than a city full of electric cars.</p>",
